7. TRAINING STRATEGY - BUILDING TRAINERS, NOT JUST ATTENDEES

A handful of founding organizers can only be in so many rooms. So the training strategy is built around a specific goal: producing local trainers who can run events on their own, in their own city.

The four levels:

Level 1, Foundations (everyone starts here). What Zcash actually is, shielded versus transparent addresses explained without jargon, setting up a first wallet safely, and basic security hygiene: seed phrases, phishing awareness, device hygiene.

Level 2, Practical Use. Sending, receiving, and shielding transactions; the Belgian and EU tools available for buying, holding, and trading; enough tax and compliance grounding to know what to bring to an accountant.

Level 3, Merchant and Business Training. Accepting ZEC as a business, point-of-sale options and settlement, and why privacy tends to build customer trust rather than complicate it.

Level 4, Train the Trainer. This is the level that makes the rest of the plan work. Members who've gone through Levels 1 to 3 and want to teach move into a certification track at the Hub, learning how to run a Level 1 session on their own, how to field the questions that come up every time (volatility, "is this legal," "what if I lose my seed phrase"), and how to represent the project fairly, risks included.

Deployment. Once someone is certified, they become the standing local contact for their own city or region, able to run:
1. A monthly Hub-style meetup in their own city, even without a permanent local Hub.
2. Their own campus's Level 1 and Level 2 sessions between our scheduled visits.
3. Local merchant conversations in their own language and neighborhood.

This is what lets the Campus Circuit and city outreach reach further than a small central team ever could alone. Brussels trains the trainers, and the trainers carry it the rest of the way.


8. RESOURCES AND KNOWLEDGE INFRASTRUCTURE

8a. ZecHub

We lean on ZecHub as an open, community-maintained knowledge base. Its wallet guides, protocol explainers, and documentation form the backbone of our Level 1 to Level 3 curriculum, localized and translated for a Belgian audience.

8b. Zcash for Belgians

Our flagship printed publication is a training handbook covering the cryptography, the market, the payments case, and the Belgian and EU compliance picture, written specifically for a reader here. See the companion editorial blueprint for the full eleven-part structure, chapter briefs, and author notes.

It carries a cover price, and will be sold both at events and through the website. A book of this depth, with named contributing experts and a real risk section, costs real money to write, edit, and print.

Alongside that, we're pursuing a grant-financed free-copy program through an application to Zcash Community Grants (ZCG). If and when that funding comes through, a batch of grant-financed copies will be available at no cost to active Community Hub members and to attendees at Campus Circuit visits, Hub meetups, and quarterly conferences, first-come first-served, limited to however many copies the grant actually covers. We'll always be clear at each event about how many free copies are left, so it's a promise we can keep rather than one we're hoping to.

Anyone outside that pool, a curious non-member or a business owner who wants to read it before committing to anything, can buy a copy directly through the website or at any event.

8c. The Zcash Belgium Website

The digital backbone tying everything above together:
1. Resource Index: a structured library of Zcash ecosystem tools, wallets, documentation, and technologies, organized by skill level.
2. Local Chapter Forum: where members discuss Zcash Belgium related topics, regional meetups, merchant recommendations, translated resources, peer support.
3. Support Desk: live community-staffed chat during Hub hours (Monday to Friday, 10:00 to 17:00 CET), ticketed support outside those hours, and a growing FAQ and knowledge base.
4. Events and Calendar Hub: the live, editable schedule for every campus stop, Hub meetup, virtual call, and conference, including free-copy availability.
5. Book Store: where Zcash for Belgians can be bought directly, with a clear note on where the proceeds go.


9. OUTREACH STRATEGY

9a. Street and City Outreach. A rolling calendar of city visits radiating out from Brussels: Antwerp, Ghent, Liege, Bruges, Leuven, Charleroi, Namur, Mons. Pop-up info tables and short, easy conversations about Zcash, always pointing back to the website and Hub calendar.

9b. University Outreach. Beyond the formal Campus Circuit residencies: partnerships with student fintech, economics, and computer science societies, poster and flyer campaigns ahead of each monthly visit, and local trainers keeping momentum alive between official visits.

9c. Business and Merchant Outreach. Direct outreach to independent shops, cafes, and service businesses, starting in high-footfall Brussels neighborhoods and expanding outward with the Campus Circuit and trainer network:
1. Free point-of-sale onboarding and staff training.
2. A first-90-days support window for any merchant who says yes.
3. A listing on the website's merchant directory once onboarded.

9d. Mall and High-Street Campaigns. Scheduled weekend activations in shopping centers and pedestrian high streets across the rotation cities: on-the-spot wallet setup, Zcash for Belgians available for purchase, and an easy way to turn a curious passerby into a website sign-up.


10. FLAGSHIP ACTIVITIES

University Meetups: the monthly, up-to-3-day Campus Circuit residency; our main engine for reaching the next generation of users and future trainers.

Zcash Belgium Conference: our quarterly flagship, hosted at rotating regional event centers, bringing businesses, investors, developers, and the wider community together for panels, workshops, and networking.

Merchant Connect: a recurring format, at conferences and standalone Hub sessions, showcasing where you can already spend ZEC in Belgium and recruiting the next wave of businesses.

