13. FUNDING AND PARTNERSHIPS

We don't want this to rest on a single source of money, so the plan draws on a few at once:

1. Zcash Community Grants (ZCG). We'll apply quarterly, specifically to help fund the events, conferences, and community meetups scheduled for that period. Separately, and on its own timeline, we're also submitting a one-time ZCG request to finance a batch of free copies of Zcash for Belgians (see Section 8b). That request is independent of the recurring quarterly one. Hub rental and day-to-day operations are funded differently again, through member donations and, over time, the Zcash-based services described below, so the Hub's running costs don't depend on either grant coming through. If donations fall short of covering that gap in a given period, closing it is exactly what the future services revenue is meant to do as the organization grows.

2. Member donations, via the website. A straightforward way for people who value what Zcash Belgium is building to support it directly, on whatever scale suits them.

3. Zcash-based services, further down the line. Once the Hub and the trainer network are established, we'd like to explore building and offering practical Zcash solutions for businesses, traders, and payments, the kind of infrastructure work merchants and investors already need help with. Done well, this could become a genuine source of income for the organization, so we're not leaning on the community for funding indefinitely. This is a future direction rather than something we're building during the foundation phase, but it's part of why we see Zcash Belgium as something that can eventually support itself.

Alongside all of this, we're open to venue partnerships, reduced-rate space at event centers and hotels in exchange for visibility, and university co-hosting, which lowers cost on both sides.


14. FOUNDATION ROADMAP (LATE 2026 TO EARLY 2027)

Zcash Belgium doesn't exist yet. This is a proposal, and nothing on this list happens without funding behind it first. So the roadmap below starts with securing that funding, and everything else, starting with the Hub itself, follows once it's in place.

We're picking this plan up in the second half of 2026, so the foundation phase runs from now through the first half of 2027, deliberately narrow, and entirely in Brussels. The national rollout described in Section 6 doesn't begin until this base is solid.

1. Q3 to Q4 2026 (August to December). Submit our first funding request to Zcash Community Grants, covering everything needed to establish a real, visible presence in Brussels within this period: renting and setting up the Hub; building and hosting the website; two campus visits and events in Brussels, starting with ULB; street and city outreach across Brussels (shopping malls, pedestrian high streets, transit hubs and train stations, public squares, and weekend markets); business and merchant outreach; initial marketing; and weekly virtual meetups, Hub walk-in office hours, and monthly in-person Hub meetups, all starting as soon as the Hub opens its doors. This is the funding groundwork the rest of the roadmap depends on.

2. Q1 2027 (January to March). Print Zcash for Belgians, covering the cost of the first run ourselves if ZCG funding for those copies hasn't come through by then; submit our next quarterly ZCG application, covering that period's events and meetups.

3. Q2 2027 (April to June). Publish Zcash for Belgians and begin selling it through the website and at events; continue deepening business and city outreach across Brussels; close out the foundation phase and finalize the plan for national rollout.


